Linked List Operations
1. Traversal

Traversal means visiting each node of the linked list one by one to access or display data.

Process
Start from the head node
Move to the next node repeatedly
Stop when NULL is reached
Example
10 → 20 → 30 → NULL

Traversal output:

10 20 30
2. Insertion

Insertion means adding a new node into the linked list.

Types of Insertion
a) Insertion at Beginning

New node is added before the head node.

b) Insertion at End

New node is added after the last node.

c) Insertion at Specific Position

New node is inserted between two nodes.

Advantage

Insertion is efficient because shifting elements is not required.

3. Deletion

Deletion means removing a node from the linked list.

Types of Deletion
a) Deletion from Beginning

Head node is removed.

b) Deletion from End

Last node is removed.

c) Deletion at Specific Position

A node between two nodes is removed.

Important Point

Pointers must be updated properly after deletion.

4. Searching

Searching means finding whether a particular element exists in the linked list.

Process
Start from head
Compare each node value with target value
Stop when value is found or list ends
Example

Searching 20 in:

10 → 20 → 30

Result:

Element Found
5. Updating

Updating means changing the value stored in a node.

Example
10 → 20 → 30

Update 20 to 25:

10 → 25 → 30
6. Reversing

Reversing means changing the direction of links.

Example

Original:

10 → 20 → 30 → NULL

Reversed:

30 → 20 → 10 → NULL
7. Sorting

Sorting arranges nodes in ascending or descending order.

Example

Before sorting:

30 → 10 → 20

After sorting:

10 → 20 → 30
8. Merging

Merging combines two linked lists into one list.

Example
List 1: 10 → 20
List 2: 30 → 40

Merged List:

10 → 20 → 30 → 40
